    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Angevin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I</a> rebuilt royal power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arpad">Árpád</a> line died out in 1301, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visegrad">Visegrád</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buda">Buda</a>.</li><li>Gold from its mines made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s leading producer of precious metals and its florin a standard coin.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a> power, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnate</a> rule followed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> crushed it at Mohács in 1526.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bibliotheca-corviniana">Bibliotheca Corviniana</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> was one of the largest libraries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>; over two hundred of its volumes survive in collections across the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

