    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#arpad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arpad.jpg" alt="Árpád" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#arpad">Árpád</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%81rp%C3%A1d_R%C3%A1ckeve.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Csanády, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> had been founded around 1000, when Stephen I of the Árpád dynasty was crowned its first Christian king. When the last male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arpad">Árpád</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andrew-iii-of-hungary">Andrew III</a>, died in January 1301, several candidates descended from the dynasty through the female line claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-hungary">Charles Robert of Anjou</a>, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mary-of-hungary-queen-of-naples">Mary of Hungary</a>, a daughter of Stephen V, was backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> but opposed first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wenceslaus-iii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus of Bohemia</a>, crowned in 1301, and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#otto-iii-duke-of-bavaria">Otto of Bavaria</a>, crowned in 1305. At the same time much of the country was controlled by great lords, the so-called oligarchs, who ruled their regions as independent princes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthew-iii-csak">Matthew Csák</a> in the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amadeus-aba">Amadeus Aba</a> in the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ladislaus-kan">Ladislaus Kán</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transylvania">Transylvania</a> and the Kőszegi family in the west. Charles was crowned three times, the last and only fully valid coronation taking place with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crown-of-thorns">Holy Crown</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a> in 1310. He then fought the oligarchs one by one, defeating the sons of Amadeus Aba at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rozgony">Battle of Rozgony</a> in 1312 and taking over Matthew Csák's lands after his death in 1321. By the early 1320s royal authority had been restored throughout the kingdom, and Charles I set up his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visegrad">Visegrád</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danube">Danube</a> bend, beginning two centuries of strong, if interrupted, royal government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kremnica"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kremnica.jpg" alt="Kremnica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kremnica">Kremnica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kremnica_v_zime.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dušan Turan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a> was a large, thinly populated kingdom of probably around three million people by 1500, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magyars">Magyars</a>, Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#romanians">Romanians</a>, Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ruthenians">Ruthenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serbs">Serbs</a>, as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transylvanian-saxons">Saxons of Transylvania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#szekelys">Szeklers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cumans">Cumans</a>, who held distinct collective rights. Most people were peasants who held land from lords and paid rents, church tithes and, from 1351, a ninth (<em>nona</em>) of their produce to their landlord. The country's great wealth lay in its mines. Gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kremnica">Kremnica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#banska-stiavnica">Banská Štiavnica</a> made Hungary the leading producer of precious metals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> for much of the fourteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I</a>'s gold florin, minted from 1325, became one of the continent's standard coins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">Cattle</a>, driven on the hoof to markets in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> were further exports, while cloth and luxuries were imported. Towns were small by western standards and often German-speaking, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buda">Buda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bratislava">Pozsony</a> (Bratislava), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kosice">Kassa</a> (Košice) and the mining towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a> the most important. The Catholic Church, led by the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#esztergom">Esztergom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kalocsa">Kalocsa</a>, owned great estates; universities were founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pecs">Pécs</a> in 1367 and at Pozsony in 1465, though neither lasted long, and the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Italian Renaissance</a> art and humanism north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alps">Alps</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nicopolis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis.jpg" alt="Battle of Nicopolis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NikopolisSchlacht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sébastien Mamerot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hungarian armies combined noble levies, the private troops (<em>banderia</em>) of the king, prelates and barons, Cuman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#szekelys">Szekler</a> light cavalry and growing numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I</a> they campaigned widely: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naples">Naples</a> in 1347–1350 to avenge the murder of his brother Andrew, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venice">Venice</a>, which ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-zadar">Treaty of Zadar</a> in 1358, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkans">Balkans</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bosnia">Bosnia</a>, Serbian lords, Wallachia and Moldavia were drawn into Hungarian suzerainty. From the 1370s the Ottoman advance became the chief threat. Sigismund's crusading army was crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a> in 1396, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wladyslaw-iii-of-poland">Vladislaus I</a> fell at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-varna">Battle of Varna</a> in 1444. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transylvania">Transylvania</a> and later governor of the kingdom, won victories in the Long Campaign of 1443–1444 and lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-battle-of-kosovo">Second Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1448, but in July 1456 broke the Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belgrade">Belgrade</a> with the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-of-capistrano">John of Capistrano</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> created a standing mercenary force later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-army-of-hungary">Black Army</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, Hussite-style wagon forts and handgunners, which conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vienna">Vienna</a>, while his commanders defeated an Ottoman raid at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-breadfield">Battle of Breadfield</a> in 1479. After the Black Army was disbanded in the 1490s the southern fortress line decayed, Belgrade fell in 1521, and on 29 August 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> destroyed the Hungarian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#golden-bull-of-1222"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of 1222" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:II._Andr%C3%A1s_aranybull%C3%A1ja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Angevin kings rebuilt royal power on new foundations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I</a> rewarded loyal barons with offices, castles and their revenues rather than outright grants of land, reformed the coinage and mining royalties, and introduced a household tax, the 'chamber's profit' (<em>lucrum camerae</em>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I</a>'s laws of 1351 confirmed the liberties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a>, declared all nobles equal in rights and introduced entail (<em>aviticitas</em>), which kept noble land within families. Under Sigismund (r. 1387–1437), who also became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, the royal council and chancery grew, while the leading barons shared power through leagues and the Order of the Dragon, founded in 1408. In the fifteenth century the crown became elective in practice, and the diet, an assembly of prelates, barons, nobles and royal towns, gained the right to elect kings and approve taxes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> (r. 1458–1490), elected by the nobility in 1458, centralised government through a professional chancery staffed by lesser nobles and clerics, raised extraordinary taxes nearly every year and reformed the treasury in 1467. Under the Jagiellonian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vladislaus II</a> and Louis II the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a> regained control and royal revenues collapsed, while the lawyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#istvan-werboczy">István Werbőczy</a> compiled the customary law of the nobility in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tripartitum">Tripartitum</a></em> of 1514, which remained the basis of Hungarian noble law until 1848.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#holy-crown-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Holy Crown of Hungary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Parliament_002_-_Flickr_-_granada_turnier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">granada_turnier, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For two centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a> was one of the great powers of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and the main bulwark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin-christendom">Latin Christendom</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Its kings were linked by marriage and personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and the meeting of the kings of Hungary, Bohemia and Poland at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visegrad">Visegrád</a> in 1335 is remembered as an early example of central European cooperation, the namesake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visegrad-group">Visegrád Group</a> founded in 1991. Hungarian gold underpinned European coinage, and the Hungarian florin was imitated across the region. Hunyadi's defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1456 was celebrated throughout Europe, and the noon bell ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-callixtus-iii">Pope Callixtus III</a> to call for prayers for the Christian forces became associated with the victory. The court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buda">Buda</a> and Visegrád was one of the first centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Italian Renaissance</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>: Italian humanists, architects and artists worked there, and the royal library, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bibliotheca-corviniana">Bibliotheca Corviniana</a>, held perhaps two thousand volumes, of which over two hundred survive and are listed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World</a> register. The kingdom's institutions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown</a> as the symbol of the state, the diet and the noble privileges codified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#istvan-werboczy">Werbőczy</a>, shaped Hungarian political life into the modern era, and its memory as a lost golden age, ended at Mohács, became central to Hungarian national identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs.jpg" alt="Battle of Mohács" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_moh%C3%A1csi_csata_(Des_T%C3%BCrckischen_Keysers_Solymon_schlacht_-_So_mit_dem_K%C3%BCnig_von_Hungern_gethan,_Bamberg,_1526).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The collapse came quickly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias</a>'s death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vladislaus II</a>, already king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bohemia">Bohemia</a>, in 1490 largely because he would not rule as Matthias had; the unpaid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-army-of-hungary">Black Army</a> was disbanded in the early 1490s, the treasury dwindled, and the southern fortresses fell into disrepair. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-pressburg-1491">Treaty of Pressburg</a> of 1491 promised the succession to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> if the Jagiellonian line died out, and a double marriage agreement of 1515 tied the two dynasties together. In 1514 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">crusade</a> called against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> turned into a peasant uprising under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gyorgy-dozsa">György Dózsa</a>, which was crushed with great cruelty; Dózsa was executed, and the diet bound the peasants permanently to the land. Under the boy-king Louis II (r. 1516–1526) factions of magnates and lesser nobles feuded while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1521. In 1526 Suleiman advanced up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danube">Danube</a>, and on 29 August his much larger army annihilated the Hungarian forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>; Louis II drowned while fleeing, and many prelates and barons were killed. The Ottomans briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buda">Buda</a> and then withdrew. Two rival kings were elected, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> in November and Ferdinand I of Habsburg in December 1526, and after the Ottoman seizure of Buda in 1541 the kingdom was divided into Habsburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-hungary">Ottoman Hungary</a> and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transylvania">Transylvania</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a>, rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a> and enriched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s richest gold mines, was a leading central European power and the chief barrier against Ottoman expansion; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> it became an early northern home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a>, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnate</a> rule and defeat at Mohács in 1526 destroyed it.</em></p>

